---
const rows = [
  ["who", "The girl who found the map smiled.", "Which girl."],
  ["which", "The map, which was torn, showed a lake.", "More about the map."],
  ["that", "The book that I borrowed is excellent.", "Which book."],
  ["whose", "The boy whose bike broke walked home.", "Which boy, and whose bike."],
  ["where", "The village where we stayed was quiet.", "Which village."],
  ["when", "The day when we met was sunny.", "Which day."]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Clauses - Relative clauses</h1>
  <p>
    A <strong>relative clause</strong> is a subordinate clause that gives information about a noun. The noun
    it describes is its <strong>antecedent</strong>.
  </p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-lilac">
    <h2>It tells us which one</h2>
    <p>The cyclist <strong>who wore a yellow helmet</strong> waved.</p>
    <p>
      <em>Who wore a yellow helmet</em> tells us which cyclist. Its verb is <strong>wore</strong>. It belongs
      next to <em>the cyclist</em>. The main clause is “The cyclist waved.”
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-sand">
    <h2>Common openings</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Opening</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Example</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">It explains</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map(([word, example, explains]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{word}</th>
              <td>{example}</td>
              <td>{explains}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <p>
    The boy <strong>with a red scarf</strong> is a phrase. The boy <strong>who wore a red scarf</strong> is a
    relative clause, because it has the verb <strong>wore</strong>.
  </p>
</div>
